{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point","id":867157,"title":"[2ed] Dominate and Breaking Point","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-17T16:34:27-07:00","id":867157,"title":"[2ed] Dominate and Breaking Point","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Experience a breaking point when following the command, and succeed at the related Resolve + Composure roll. </div> </div> </div>Hi all.<br /> What exactly means:&quot;Experience a breaking point when following the command&quot; in order to resolve the Dominated (or mesmerized) condition? The act that causes the breaking point should be executed or the breking point incurs before the act, awekening the dominated from this condition?<br /> <br /> I've always thought that the breaking point incurs after the act. For example: &quot;Kill that man&quot;. The dominated shoots with his gun to the man pointed and after this (killed or not) he take the breaking point and resolve the mesmerized condition. In fact: &quot;Shoot your husband&quot; is an example of suitable command according to the power description.<br /> But my doubt now is: what in the Dominate mechanics do not allow the victim to commit suicide? Why a command like: &quot;Shoot yourself in the head&quot; or &quot;Jump out the window&quot; (at the tenth floor), shouldn't works?<br /> There is a sentence in the text or some aspect of the mechanics that I missed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 04-17-2016, 07:45 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-17T17:02:42-07:00","id":867180,"title":"","content":"In my opionion, it stands by reason that the Breaking Point happen after the commanded act or else dominate would be totally useless against most people. And also it's in the description with the shoot you husband example. If you couldnt do that why would it be an example? Dominate 1 allows for really evil and hard commands like &quot;jump off that cliff&quot; and sure the victim will jump. So yes it will work with suicide commands even though in most situations it's hard to pull off (and most likely a detatcmnhent roll against murder) cus the condition 'mesmerized' is really easy to break. It's pretty hard actually to kill oneself in one turn by RAW for most people. Though ' Shoot your own head' dosnt really need you to blow you brain out and kill yourself.<br /> But every situation must be veiwed and interpreted, but yes Dominate 1 can by principle kill a man by suicide.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/523-poseur","name":"Poseur","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar523_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"375"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T13:18:24-07:00","id":867768,"title":"","content":"Killing yourself wouldn't work, reaching a breaking would but you would snap out of Dominate. For imstance &quot;Kill that guy and bring me some kookies&quot; you would kill the man, then reach a breaking point, feeling miserable about what you did and wouldn't pursue the command","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1851-jps","name":"JPS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T14:49:32-07:00","id":867834,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JPS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867768#post867768\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Killing yourself wouldn't work, reaching a breaking would but you would snap out of Dominate. For imstance &amp;quot;Kill that guy and bring me some kookies&amp;quot; you would kill the man, then reach a breaking point, feeling miserable about what you did and wouldn't pursue the command</div> </div> </div> </div>Bearing in mind that a lot of things about Integrity and breaking points are subjective, I don't think this uniformly bears out. In fact, I'd argue for the vast majority of well adjusted people, attempting to kill someone or getting into the situation of trying to kill them will cause a breaking point, which will make them resolve it.<br /> <br /> For the hardened killer or assassin, killing somebody is still a breaking point, but I'd wager that attempting to kill someone is not, meaning they might be able to just do it, then snap out of it when they check for the murder.<br /> <br /> It's similar to suicide. Actually committing suicide isn't a breaking point because you're dead. Attempting suicide is certainly a breaking point, which is why You'd need more than Dominate 1 to convince someone to off themselves knowingly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:16:11-07:00","id":867852,"title":"","content":"Yes but given that &quot;shoot your husband&quot; is implied to be a valid and useful command, we have to conclude that in most cases the dominated guy will shoot and then resolve the condition. Sure, if there is a gun <i>right there</i> and you tell them to &quot;<i>kill</i> [their] husband&quot; the husband is only getting shot once and then the condition will be resolved, no matter how much he is actually hurt. But in both cases, he' getting shot.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://dl.dropboxusercontent.com/u/7019410/Images/Ex3%20KS%20Badge.gif\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1866-ophion","name":"Ophion","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:23:53-07:00","id":867855,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ophion</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867852#post867852\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes but given that &quot;shoot your husband&quot; is implied to be a valid and useful command, we have to conclude that in most cases the dominated guy will shoot and then resolve the condition. Sure, if there is a gun <i>right there</i> and you tell them to &quot;<i>kill</i> [their] husband&quot; the husband is only getting shot once and then the condition will be resolved, no matter how much he is actually hurt. But in both cases, he' getting shot.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's valid in the sense that you can ask it, not in the sense that it will be carried out. Mesmerize can order someone to do it, but if attempting to harm their wife is a breaking point, which again, I wager it is for <i>most people</i>, then Dominate 1 won't cut it. Now, in the same situation, if you use Iron Edict, he has to succeed the Integrity check if he wants to avoid shooting his wife. Same for if you order him to shoot himself.<br /> <br /> Iron Edict is where the money is at, people. They have to succeed the check in order to not do what you want, while Mesmerize just needs them to test for the breaking point to resolve.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:24:29-07:00","id":867856,"title":"","content":"It's not readily apparent in the rules but I think it's clearly intended that low level Dominate cannot force someone to knowingly commit suicide considering complete mastery of Dominate only allows you to commit suicide while possessing someone if they cannot pass a Resolve+BP check, and if they pass it the possessing vampire is forced out.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9368-princeraven","name":"PrinceRaven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:25:49-07:00","id":867858,"title":"","content":"Probably I'm missing something about the breaking point mechanic. <br /> I thought that the breaking point occurred after the character had done the action. Some example taken from the book: at humanity 2 &quot;premeditated killing&quot; is a breaking point. I read it as you kill someone and THEN incur in the breaking point, not before. Am I wrong?<br /> Why attempting to take an action like a suicide should be a breaking point? In my point of view, you try to suicide (for example shooting to your head or jumping out the windows), then, even if you don't die, you incur in the breaking point for the ACTION attempted and resolve the condition. <br /> I always seen breaking point as result of an action. If I'm wrong help me to understand <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> I really think that Dominate is a little overpower in this way but my question is if the rules are according to my interpretation or I am wrong.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:30:37-07:00","id":867865,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>PrinceRaven</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867856#post867856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not readily apparent in the rules but I think it's clearly intended that low level Dominate cannot force someone to knowingly commit suicide considering complete mastery of Dominate only allows you to commit suicide while possessing someone if they cannot pass a Resolve+BP check, and if they pass it the possessing vampire is forced out.</div> </div> </div> </div>Given that the Resolve+BP check is actually less likely to succeed than a Resolve + Composure check for a mortal, I'm not so sure that's the thing to take away from it. It's clearly intended to give a last recourse to the Possessed victim as one thing, and I'm fairly certain that either succeed the Breaking Point or suffering some damage from a suicide attempt both resolving the Dominated Condition resemble this in giving recourse to the suicide victim or the the guy about to kill somebody.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Also worth mentioning that, given how tough it is to kill Kindred in general, they're almost certain to either succeed whatever check they might make to not do it, or resolve Dominated from the damage they take. If one of them shoots them self in the head with a shotgun, it might do like, 4+ Bashing Damage, which being in excess of most Kindred's stamina, will almost certainly trigger and resolve it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-18-2016, 06:33 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:34:24-07:00","id":867867,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867855#post867855\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> It's valid in the sense that you can ask it, not in the sense that it will be carried out. Mesmerize can order someone to do it, but if attempting to harm their wife is a breaking point, which again, I wager it is for <i>most people</i>, then Dominate 1 won't cut it. Now, in the same situation, if you use Iron Edict, he has to succeed the Integrity check if he wants to avoid shooting his wife. Same for if you order him to shoot himself.<br /> <br /> Iron Edict is where the money is at, people. They have to succeed the check in order to not do what you want, while Mesmerize just needs them to test for the breaking point to resolve.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly I think that killing anyone is a breaking point for the most part of the human and even vampire, so it's hard for me to think that they put this example in the description of the Mesmerize power as a valid command without spending a word to clarify that for the most part of the people this command wouldn't work.<br /> I think, instead, that the difference between Mesmerize and Iron edict actually is that, in the first case you shoot your wife and resolve the condition, in the second case you shoot your wife and, if you fail the check roll don't resolve the condition, so if the command ask to you for something else, you go on to fallow it.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Why not a FAQ on this question? :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T15:38:39-07:00","id":867873,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867867#post867867\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Honestly I think that killing anyone is a breaking point for the most part of the human and even vampire, so it's hard for me to think that they put this example in the description of the Mesmerize power as a valid command without spending a word to clarify that for the most part of the people this command wouldn't work.<br /> I think, instead, that the difference between Mesmerize and Iron edict actually is that, in the first case you shoot your wife and resolve the condition, in the second case you shoot your wife and, if you fail the check roll don't resolve the condition, so if the command ask to you for something else, you go on to fallow it.</div> </div> </div> </div>The command will work always, because it is always a valid command for Mesmerize. Whether <b>Mesmerized</b>, the Condition, will resolve automatically because it is a breaking point is a separate issue from that entirely. Reiterating this other point again: Killing someone is always a breaking point in Integrity, but <i><b>attempting</b> to kill someone may <b>not</b> be for a given person. </i><br /> <br /> Lastly, keep in mind the difference between Mesmerize and Iron Edict is spending a Vitae. Nothing else. Iron Edict is like an aftermarket mod on your mesmerism. You add it to ensure that the command will be carried out, regardless of the victim's moral constitution.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T16:54:43-07:00","id":867913,"title":"","content":"I would argue that until you pull the trigger, you haven't actually attempted to kill anyone. In general, I would assume that mesmerize doesn't ever affect when you would normally roll a breaking point, so the question becomes whether or not the ST would normally require one of their players to roll for a Breaking Point for attempting to kill someone before they actually tried to go through with it or before the attempt was made.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 04-18-2016, 07:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T17:41:17-07:00","id":867949,"title":"","content":"It's all about eyeballing it. Mesmerized is fragile when you ask someone to do something seriously compromising. Iron Edict is significantly less so. If you think that the guy being told to shoot his wife couldn't even fathom doing it, it merits a breaking point, since that's the whole point of the provision being in the power. With Mesmerize, they'll snap out of it. With Iron Edict, there's a chance that they won't until it's too late.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T17:55:56-07:00","id":867965,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867949#post867949\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's all about eyeballing it. Mesmerized is fragile when you ask someone to do something seriously compromising. Iron Edict is significantly less so. If you think that the guy being told to shoot his wife couldn't even fathom doing it, it merits a breaking point, since that's the whole point of the provision being in the power. With Mesmerize, they'll snap out of it. With Iron Edict, there's a chance that they won't until it's too late.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think I'd call bullshit in most situations if someone suffered a Breaking Point for even <i>thinking</i> about killing their wife. We're up in Integrity 9-10 territory, and I've always found that meeting someone like that should be super rare, especially in the world of darkness. Like, it <i>can</i> happen, but this shouldn't be the type of thing you come across more than like once a story.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T18:54:14-07:00","id":868034,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867965#post867965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I'd call bullshit in most situations if someone suffered a Breaking Point for even <i>thinking</i> about killing their wife.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're misunderstanding me. I'm not talking about thinking about it. I'm talking about attempting it. I mean standing over her while she's asleep in bed, knife in hand. That situation is very likely to be a breaking point for your average joe.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=867965#post867965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We're up in Integrity 9-10 territory, and I've always found that meeting someone like that should be super rare, especially in the world of darkness. Like, it <i>can</i> happen, but this shouldn't be the type of thing you come across more than like once a story.</div> </div> </div> </div>Integrity breaking points are not tied to the numerical value of your Integrity. What's a breaking point at Integrity 10 is the same as what's a breaking point at Integrity 1. Integrity is not morality, and what I'm talking about is not a moral or immoral action, but rather the personal integrity question of people, which everyone has. I'd wager that the people who would suffer a breaking point in that situation outnumber those who wouldn't.<br /> <br /> Anyway, one thing to keep in mind: A breaking point is just a test. The difference between a soldier who has trained to see combat but hasn't actually been in a violent situation and a civilian who hasn't isn't whether they make the check or not: Both are almost certainly making the check, but the soldier's training might give him the bonus dice that make it easier for him to succeed.<br /> <br /> Just the same, just because you experience a breaking point when you're standing over your wife deciding to murder her (if you're looking for a moment to tie this to, I'd tie it to the declaration of Intent) doesn't mean you <i>don't </i>murder her. It just means it's a very shocking and potentially traumatic thing to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T19:13:30-07:00","id":868046,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868034#post868034\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're misunderstanding me. I'm not talking about thinking about it. I'm talking about attempting it. I mean standing over her while she's asleep in bed, knife in hand. That situation is very likely to be a breaking point for your average joe.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Integrity breaking points are not tied to the numerical value of your Integrity. What's a breaking point at Integrity 10 is the same as what's a breaking point at Integrity 1. Integrity is not morality, and what I'm talking about is not a moral or immoral action, but rather the personal integrity question of people, which everyone has. I'd wager that the people who would suffer a breaking point in that situation outnumber those who wouldn't.<br /> <br /> Anyway, one thing to keep in mind: A breaking point is just a test. The difference between a soldier who has trained to see combat but hasn't actually been in a violent situation and a civilian who hasn't isn't whether they make the check or not: Both are almost certainly making the check, but the soldier's training might give him the bonus dice that make it easier for him to succeed.<br /> <br /> Just the same, just because you experience a breaking point when you're standing over your wife deciding to murder her (if you're looking for a moment to tie this to, I'd tie it to the declaration of Intent) doesn't mean you <i>don't </i>murder her. It just means it's a very shocking and potentially traumatic thing to do.</div> </div> </div> </div>I disagree, just standing over them isn't actually doing anything, not even attempting. Let me ask it like this, assuming Dominate wasn't being used and this was happening naturally, would you first ask the person doing this to roll for a Breaking point before attempting to commit murder and then if they decided to do it anyways immediately have them roll another for actually going through with the act? Long story short, do you have your players roll twice for every Breaking Point, for being there, ready to do it, and for the act itself? If not, the only reason to do it now is to intentionally add difficulty to the power in question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T19:51:54-07:00","id":868083,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868046#post868046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree, just standing over them isn't actually doing anything, not even attempting. Let me ask it like this, assuming Dominate wasn't being used and this was happening naturally, would you first ask the person doing this to roll for a Breaking point before attempting to commit murder and then if they decided to do it anyways immediately have them roll another for actually going through with the act?</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on how they answered the integrity questions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868046#post868046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Long story short, do you have your players roll twice for every Breaking Point, for being there, ready to do it, and for the act itself? If not, the only reason to do it now is to intentionally add difficulty to the power in question.</div> </div> </div> </div>They're not the same breaking point, but if you answered the question &quot;What's the worst thing your character can imagine himself doing?&quot; and the answer was 'harming my wife' I would certainly make you roll for harming her, and for killing her.<br /> <br /> Nevermind that this is a question to think foremost about with NPCs, who by design might have more milder breaking points than those most PCs might have, but that comes down to individual characters, like all breaking points.<br /> <br /> I think it's worth seriously noodling over what the <i>intent </i>of differentiating between the Mesmerized Condition and the Dominated Condition is. If there was no intent to differentiate the two, then the distinction between running into a breaking point and rolling it and succeeding, there'd be very little need to make different Conditions at all. Mesmerized is there for getting people to do things that aren't breaking points: Dominated is there to get them to do it even if it is one.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Another way to conceptualize what I'm getting at: For <i>some people </i>like regular people, the <i>act </i>of doing something is a different breaking point from the <i>result </i>of that action. A character who you decided is very orderly and likes breaking the rules might experience a breaking point when he realizes he <i>has </i>to break the law to survive, and he'll probably also experience one when he's <i>actually doing that</i>.<br /> <br /> Another example of this principle: Witnessing something supernatural might be a breaking point for a character. Being attacked by said supernatural thing and injured is a <i>different breaking point</i>. See Breaking Point modifiers as a place where this is clearly conceptualized, and do try to keep in mind that breaking points are actually a much more fluid concept than you give them credit for, and the questions certainly support that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-18-2016, 10:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T20:09:02-07:00","id":868092,"title":"","content":"Yeah, some people are going to be traumatized by having to point a gun at somebody, even without pulling a trigger, because it's a very violent act in itself.<br /> I'm fine with stuff like &quot;hurt your wife&quot; not working. Makes playing Ventrue more interesting - adds another layer to your strategy, as some victims will be better for certain tasks, and sometimes you will have to erode someone in order to be able to use your planned manuever on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T20:11:03-07:00","id":868094,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868046#post868046\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I disagree, just standing over them isn't actually doing anything, not even attempting. Let me ask it like this, assuming Dominate wasn't being used and this was happening naturally, would you first ask the person doing this to roll for a Breaking point before attempting to commit murder and then if they decided to do it anyways immediately have them roll another for actually going through with the act? Long story short, do you have your players roll twice for every Breaking Point, for being there, ready to do it, and for the act itself? If not, the only reason to do it now is to intentionally add difficulty to the power in question.</div> </div> </div> </div>Never been in a situation where you could have done something bad/horrible and decided against in the last second? It's like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/576-ephsy","name":"Ephsy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar576_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2615"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T20:25:25-07:00","id":868106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868083#post868083\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Depends on how they answered the integrity questions.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> They're not the same breaking point, but if you answered the question &quot;What's the worst thing your character can imagine himself doing?&quot; and the answer was 'harming my wife' I would certainly make you roll for harming her, and for killing her.<br /> <br /> Nevermind that this is a question to think foremost about with NPCs, who by design might have more milder breaking points than those most PCs might have, but that comes down to individual characters, like all breaking points.<br /> <br /> I think it's worth seriously noodling over what the <i>intent </i>of differentiating between the Mesmerized Condition and the Dominated Condition is. If there was no intent to differentiate the two, then the distinction between running into a breaking point and rolling it and succeeding, there'd be very little need to make different Conditions at all. Mesmerized is there for getting people to do things that aren't breaking points: Dominated is there to get them to do it even if it is one.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Another way to conceptualize what I'm getting at: For <i>some people </i>like regular people, the <i>act </i>of doing something is a different breaking point from the <i>result </i>of that action. A character who you decided is very orderly and likes breaking the rules might experience a breaking point when he realizes he <i>has </i>to break the law to survive, and he'll probably also experience one when he's <i>actually doing that</i>.<br /> <br /> Another example of this principle: Witnessing something supernatural might be a breaking point for a character. Being attacked by said supernatural thing and injured is a <i>different breaking point</i>. See Breaking Point modifiers as a place where this is clearly conceptualized, and do try to keep in mind that breaking points are actually a much more fluid concept than you give them credit for, and the questions certainly support that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, I think in this case it is more important to think of what the developers intended when they created the Breaking Point system. Do you think that it was their intent that every individual aspect of an act would cause a breaking point? Lets take a premeditated murder for example. Do you think they intended for a character to have to make a Breaking Point roll when they decided to kill someone, when they drove to the store to purchase a gun, again when they got to the counter, once again when they loaded the weapon, a 5th Breaking Point when they walked to their victims home, and 6th when the person opened the door, another when they drew the weapon, another for each individual time they had to pull the trigger, and then a final one for when the person was dead? <br /> <br /> Personally, I really doubt that was the intention. I'm fairly sure it was meant to be one roll for the entire affair, with modifiers based around what they experienced. So instead of a dozen Breaking Point rolls for killing one person there would only be one heavily modified roll after the deed was done, with the severity determined by how far along the character got.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T20:26:15-07:00","id":868109,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868092#post868092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yeah, some people are going to be traumatized by having to point a gun at somebody, even without pulling a trigger, because it's a very violent act in itself.<br /> <b>I'm fine with stuff like &quot;hurt your wife&quot; not working. </b>Makes playing Ventrue more interesting - adds another layer to your strategy, as some victims will be better for certain tasks, and sometimes you will have to erode someone in order to be able to use your planned manuever on them.</div> </div> </div> </div>While we're agreeing I just want to complicate this a tiny bit by pointing out the bolded, because it's not that it <i>won't </i>work. It's that you <i>can't</i> do it with <b>Mesmerized </b>but you <i>can </i>with <b>Dominated </b>if they fail the Resolve + Composure roll.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, I think in this case it is more important to think of what the developers intended when they created the Breaking Point system. Do you think that it was their intent that every individual aspect of an act would cause a breaking point? Lets take a premeditated murder for example. Do you think they intended for a character to have to make a Breaking Point roll when they decided to kill someone, when they drove to the store to purchase a gun, again when they got to the counter, once again when they loaded the weapon, a 5th Breaking Point when they walked to their victims home, and 6th when the person opened the door, another when they drew the weapon, another for each individual time they had to pull the trigger, and then a final one for when the person was dead?<br /> <br /> Personally, I really doubt that was the intention. I'm fairly sure it was meant to be one roll for the entire affair, with modifiers based around what they experienced. So instead of a dozen Breaking Point rolls for killing one person there would only be one heavily modified roll after the deed was done, with the severity determined by how far along the character got.</div> </div> </div> </div>Okay, so I really have to take issue with this, because you're misconstruing what I'm saying either deliberately or because you're not grokking what I mean. I'll try to break it down.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually, I think in this case it is more important to think of what the developers intended when they created the Breaking Point system. Do you think that it was their intent that every individual aspect of an act would cause a breaking point?</div> </div> </div> </div>You're not understanding what I'm saying. The Breaking Point system is inherently personalized, and that means it's complicated by personal breaking points for every character. Rather than misunderstanding what I'm saying, try to understand what I'm discussing, because it's just two things: The <i>act </i>and the <i>result</i>. For every situation, there are characters who may experience breaking points because of <i>the act </i>and who will also <i>probably </i>experience one because of the <i>result.</i><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lets take a premeditated murder for example. Do you think they intended for a character to have to make a Breaking Point roll when they decided to kill someone, when they drove to the store to purchase a gun, again when they got to the counter, once again when they loaded the weapon, a 5th Breaking Point when they walked to their victims home, and 6th when the person opened the door, another when they drew the weapon, another for each individual time they had to pull the trigger, and then a final one for when the person was dead?</div> </div> </div> </div>So the versatility of the system allows that you <i>could </i>have any of these be breaking points. You could have <i>all </i>of them be breaking points. You could have only one of them be breaking points. It should and will vary based upon 1. the character and their own personalized breaking points, 2. the drama of the situation. Breaking Points are dramatic, and if it's not <i>both of these things </i>then you shouldn't roll for the breaking point. So with that in mind, let's look at each of the points you make and address them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Do you think they intended for a character to have to make a Breaking Point roll when they decided to kill someone</div> </div> </div> </div>Is this a character who was imagined to have never killed anybody or not been in situations where the possibility exists? Is it a dramatic moment of realization, that they feel they have no choice but to kill the person? If so, then that sounds both appropriate and dramatic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">when they drove to the store to purchase a gun</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe they have an aversion to gun violence? They've shied away from guns since they almost killed their sibling, and by buying this gun, which they need to do this, it's a dramatic and interesting situation? If so, then yes, make the check.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">again when they got to the counter, once again when they loaded the weapon,</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a pretty arbitrary division of time, but yes, you could have one. At this point, we're getting into the territory where it feels like the point is being deliberately misconstrued rather than seriously evaluated.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868106#post868106\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">a 5th Breaking Point when they walked to their victims home, and 6th when the person opened the door, another when they drew the weapon, another for each individual time they had to pull the trigger, and then a final one for when the person was dead?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think we're again getting lost in a pedantic exploration of the point rather than a serious one. If you're the storyteller, use your good judgement. Having a breaking point for committing the B&amp;E makes a ton of sense if a character doesn't commit a lot of crimes/hasn't in the past, and can be a great way to slap on a Condition like Shaken or Spooked to incentivize the player to really explore how psychologically difficult this act might actually be.<br /> <br /> After that, we're back to the simple situation. If we're declaring the Intent to kill the person in combat, that could certainly be a great place to put a Breaking Point. It's the moment of dramatic confrontation, the climax of this little drama of a normal person deciding that another person needs to die. You know when the guy holding the gun on his victim is shaking slightly like he's nervous, hesitating before actually killing somebody, allowing them to struggle for the gun and create that situation? Yup. He had to check for a breaking point for initiating the <i>act </i>of trying to kill this person, maybe picked up the <b>Shaken </b>Condition. The player sees an awesome opportunity for drama and decides to use it to fail his check to down &amp; dirty combat kill his victim, because he has a gun trained on him, and the ST decides to narrate it as hesitation. They grapple, roll around, roll dice, until eventually, he gets enough successes and makes a Damage move, shooting the victim on the ground and ending his life. As he stares into the lifeless eyes of his victim, he realizes what he has done, and has to make another Breaking Point check. Maybe he succeeds or fails, but the ST decides to give him <b>Guilty </b>as the guy ruminates on the consequences of his actions, and wonders who he can trust with this secret.<br /> <br /> ***<br /> <br /> So yes, any one of those places could be a breaking point depending on the character. It'll depend on the character, and while maybe one of them might be a breaking point, if you're not feeling that they're significant character beats for the character in question, it'll come down to the final moment of acting and the result. The <i>act </i>and the <i>result </i>representing both sensible breaking points for a normal person, and also dramatic, interesting places to engage the system.<br /> <br /> Also I guess it can interface with <b>Mesmerized</b> and <b>Dominated</b>. And stuff.<br /> <br /> Breaking Points and Integrity are not legalistic, universal rules of conduct. They're dramatic devices which work to service character exploration. They're tools for the storyteller and the players to collaborate on exploring a character, both PCs and NPCs, and they're only necessary insofar as they serve to express and create that drama.<br /> <br /> Do you feel that it's a dramatic point where a character would struggle against mental domination when ordered to kill their beloved wife? I think so, hence why I'd suggest a breaking point for the act. It allows for that dramatic situation where the character who was <b>Dominated </b>has a chance to struggle free and turn on his would-be master. That's pretty dramatic and cool.<br /> <br /> And if he's <b>Mesmerized</b>, then he won't have to make the check. You're just the fool that didn't realize that your command of Domination was too weak to force him to go through with it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-19-2016, 12:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T21:12:38-07:00","id":868153,"title":"","content":"As a bonus point from &quot;gameplay engineering side&quot;, jumping from Mesmerized to Dominated is really felt due to things like wife thing mentioned above - and having quality of live improvements like these make Domination a very interesting experience over all levels of play. I know people might not believe me, but ability that has a restriction you have to play with for some time, and then later rewards you with lifting that restriction is &quot;scientifically&quot; more fun than just having this ability without restrictions from the start - overcoming the restriction brings psychological satisfaction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T21:25:08-07:00","id":868157,"title":"","content":"Honestly I do not agree because someone can stand in front of his wife with a gun on the point to shoot her and, at last moment, don't have the courage to do it, preserving his integrity/humanity. If he trigger the gun, otherwise, he performs the terrible act that take him to hit the breaking point loosing his morality/humanity (if he fail the check).<br /> If just thinking to do a bad action carry to a breaking point, well everyone should be at morality 1 I think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T21:43:55-07:00","id":868170,"title":"","content":"People don't seriously think about murdering others on daily basis. And yes, people do go unstable when exposed to real and touchable idea of really hurting someone. One of reasons why neurotic people prone to violent phases have it hard is because knowing that they can go hurt someone emotionally is painful in itself, and brings tons of guilt.<br /> Likewise, having sexual fantasies and feeling attraction to someone can also be a breaking point, even if you don't act on it. A faithful husband might get a breaking point for lusting after his secretary even if he doesn't act on it, and a gay youth in a homophobic country might encounter it every time he feels attraction.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","user":"WHW","date":". 04-19-2016, 12:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T22:02:30-07:00","id":868183,"title":"","content":"...remember that Integrity is not Morality. Breaking Points are not Sins. They measure how much it shakes up and disturbs you. Teen raised in a Catholic family could get a Breaking Point for downloading porn (even if he decides to erase it without watching), a serial killer could be very high Integrity person.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T22:55:43-07:00","id":868207,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"159\" data-vbnamecard=\"159\">Leetsepeak</a> - I don't think we're going to agree with how these things apply to Dominate. Simply put the book clearly indicates that using it to command someone to kill someone close to them is a valid use of the power, without any of the stipulations you seem to be wanting to add to it. The book as written is seemingly going on the assumption that this is a normal part of what you can do purely with the mesmerized condition in normal every day use. Had it not been completely feasible to do so then 'shoot your husband' wouldn't have been in the select few example phrases.<br /> <br /> But going by your interpretation, such a thing would hardly ever work due to the condition itself instantly causing a breaking point in 90% of the population and canceling itself out. I don't believe that that was the writers intention given the text that we have access to, and as such, firmly believe that your interpretation of the rules is wrong. Of course, you will believe what you will and I don't have time to try to convince you otherwise. So we're going to have to agree to disagree here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-18T23:21:53-07:00","id":868216,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868207#post868207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"159\" data-vbnamecard=\"159\">Leetsepeak</a> - I don't think we're going to agree with how these things apply to Dominate. Simply put the book clearly indicates that using it to command someone to kill someone close to them is a valid use of the power, without any of the stipulations you seem to be wanting to add to it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dude, you keep saying this as if I disagree. It's in the text, it's a valid use of the power. I'm not talking about whether you can command someone to do it or not, I'm only talking about the Condition <b>Mesmerized </b>and how it functions, or more specifically, how it resolves. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868207#post868207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The book as written is seemingly going on the assumption that this is a normal part of what you can do purely with the mesmerized condition in normal every day use. Had it not been completely feasible to do so then 'shoot your husband' wouldn't have been in the select few example phrases.</div> </div> </div> </div>The only assumption the book seems to be going on is that &quot;Shoot your husband&quot; is a command that can be given in a single turn as per Mesmerize. This is true. How it interacts with <b>Mesmerized </b>and <b>Dominated </b>is the thing that bears scrutiny.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ashenrogue</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868207#post868207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But going by your interpretation, such a thing would hardly ever work due to the condition itself instantly causing a breaking point in 90% of the population and canceling itself out. I don't believe that that was the writers intention given the text that we have access to, and as such, firmly believe that your interpretation of the rules is wrong. Of course, you will believe what you will and I don't have time to try to convince you otherwise. So we're going to have to agree to disagree here.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm hoping that this time will be the time that what I'm saying gets through to you, since you keep deciding what my interpretation is rather than reading it. <br /> <br /> So let me make it clear: <b>Mesmerized is a Condition that resolves when someone encounters a breaking point. Dominated is a Condition that resolves if they succeed the Resolve + Composure roll, but does not resolve if they fail it. Ergo, the intended function is that Mesmerize, the 'low level' effect of Dominate, is the kind you use when the action is either of little consequence or is unlikely to lead to a breaking point. If you want to order somebody to kill your wife, you use Iron Edict, which modifies Mesmerize by spending 1 Vitae, because unlike Mesmerized, the Dominated Condition does not automatically resolve when they encounter the breaking point. Since the command 'shoot your husband' is under the limit described in Mesmerize, it may be applied in the 1 turn required to give a command. That is the value of placing it in the Mesmerized section, it is an example of a command that can be given by the 1 dot effect of Dominate, which does not at all engage with what happens when the Condition of Mesmerized is applied to a person due to their personal breaking points. If this difference didn't exist between the Mesmerized and Dominated Conditions, there would be no point to having both of them and describing them differently.</b><br /> <br /> So if it bears reiterating <b><i>again </i></b>hat I am saying is that when you want someone to do something that's a breaking point, you spend 1 Vitae and issue the command as per Iron Edict. That's the difference between the 1st dot and the 2nd dot. That is why they are different dots in the Discipline of Dominate.<br /> <br /> <b>Mesmerized</b> works all the time on the things its supposed to work against. Those situations where it doesn't work are usually the ones where you want to apply <b>Dominated</b>.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T02:47:18-07:00","id":868287,"title":"","content":"I know probably what I'm going to write wont' change our reciprocal point of view, anyway:<br /> <br /> The Chronicles of darkness book says: <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> When a character performs certain actions or endures certain experiences, he might reach a breaking point. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A breaking point can fall into one of the following<br /> categories:<br /> \u2022 The character performs an action that either violates<br /> his personal moral code or that is considered unacceptable<br /> in society.<br /> \u2022 The character witnesses something traumatic, terrifying,<br /> or that rattles his understanding of the world.<br /> \u2022 The character is the victim of a supernatural attack,<br /> whether physical, emotional, or mental. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Perform&quot; is not &quot;is on the point to do&quot; in my opinion. &quot;Endures&quot; is something that he experience as a third person.<br /> <br /> and at p.73/74<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A breaking point simply means that what a character has done or seen has outstripped his ability to rationalize or handle it. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Has Done or Seen&quot; are both verbs at the past. The character makes an action or sees something bad and then experience the breaking point. Nothing says that the breaking point can occur before the action.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T04:20:20-07:00","id":868313,"title":"","content":"I think I agree with Ashenrogue and Marcus - the Mesmerized and Dominated Condition break when you experience a breaking point, and breaking points happen with performing certain actions. I don't believe that the Condition would break for anything short of following through on the action. The conditions both specifically say &quot;experience a breaking point.&quot; Not encounter a breaking point.<br /> <br /> That said, with the situation of &quot;shoot your husband?&quot; I can see the argument that the mere act of pointing a gun at a loved one could cause a breaking point. As per CofD core, Integrity breaking points include &quot;an action that either violates his personal moral code or that is considered unacceptable in society.&quot; Pointing guns at people is generally unacceptable in society. So, that's a case where the mere act of pointing a gun could break Mesmerized / Dominated. Of course, that's not universally true for humans (merely a possibility), and pointing a gun at a someone isn't a breaking point for Humanity or many other Integrity equivalents.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T06:19:58-07:00","id":868361,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868313#post868313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think I agree with Ashenrogue and Marcus - the Mesmerized and Dominated Condition break when you experience a breaking point, and <b>breaking points happen with performing certain actions.</b> I don't believe that the Condition would break for anything short of following through on the action. The conditions both specifically say &quot;experience a breaking point.&quot; Not encounter a breaking point.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is precisely what we are all saying. When I discuss pointing a gun at your husband and you discuss killing him, we are talking about two separate breaking points, not the Condition resolving in anticipation of the latter one. I am saying that in the situation where the <i>act </i>is a breaking point that the Condition will resolve. The <i>act </i>being a breaking point is a separate breaking point from the <i>result</i>. Mesmerized will resolve whenever you hit a breaking point, so if the <i>act </i>is a breaking point for the character, it will resolve there. If the <i>act </i>is not a breaking point, then it will resolve due to the <i>result </i>being a breaking point.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868313#post868313\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That said, with the situation of &quot;shoot your husband?&quot; <b>I can see the argument that the mere act of pointing a gun at a loved one could cause a breaking point.</b> As per CofD core, Integrity breaking points include &quot;an action that either violates his personal moral code or that is considered unacceptable in society.&quot; Pointing guns at people is generally unacceptable in society. So, that's a case where the mere act of pointing a gun could break Mesmerized / Dominated. <b>Of course, that's not universally true for humans (merely a possibility)</b>, and pointing a gun at a someone isn't a breaking point for Humanity or many other Integrity equivalents.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is exactly the point. It is not universally true, but it is <b>probable </b>that the situation of threatening the life of someone you love is a breaking point for most <b>normal </b>people, hence why an average person will probably break <b>Mesmerized </b>when asked to do so. They will probably <i>not </i>break <b>Dominated</b> because they have to succeed the check to do so, hence why one uses <b>Iron Edict </b>in situations where there is a possibility someone might hesitate.<br /> <br /> If we're having a hard time grasping the concept of common breaking points for normal people, there are pretty good examples in the corebook's discussion of Integrity, where they talk about how a police detective might handle the scene of a crime versus a normal person. Due to a higher threshold for witnessing the result of violence, a police detective with years of experience probably doesn't have to make a breaking point check when he walks on the scene of a homicide. Contrast with someone who isn't around dead bodies all the time, and even seeing a dead person might be a breaking point.<br /> <br /> This is why answering the Integrity questions are pretty important for determining a player character's threshold, because if you answer that your soldier's idea of <i>what is the worst thing he could imagine himself doing? </i>is &quot;betraying his allies&quot; then the ST might rule that, because it's dramatic and appropriate, that the soldier being put in a situation where he has intent to hurt his comrades would constitute a breaking point.<br /> <br /> For NPCs the ST has to eyeball these things, but with a general eyes towards what people are capable of and normative society, I'd certainly say that pointing a gun at your spouse with intent to kill them is a breaking point for most people, because that behavior certainly qualifies as possibly violating a personal moral code, is definitely considered unacceptable in society, and the situation of being made to kill them unwillingly is certainly a traumatic experience as well.<br /> <br /> Your Storyteller has to make that call. I'd advise it for normal people, <i>maybe </i>not the mobster who kills people for a living? that the act is a breaking point in addition to the result. But yes, as we are both saying, it comes down to individual people, how that character's integrity is conceptualized and whether it is dramatically appropriate or not for the ST to check for Integrity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-19-2016, 09:33 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T10:38:04-07:00","id":868556,"title":"","content":"Obviously there are different interpretations here. Leetsepeak, I see your point about the fact that being put into a position where you're about to kill someone would stressful but, game wise it's only after you do it that you would have to make this breaking point roll and, imo, that's why &quot;kill your husband&quot; would be a valid command.<br /> Sure, breaking points could somewhat be subjective, somewhat. Because, by and large, we are the products of the society we live in when it comes to values and social norms. This must be adjusted to reflect specificities (I'm a soldier, I'm a gangster, I'm a homicide detective etc.) but I don't think that the adjustment should widly vary from one person to another if they both belong to the same subculture.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1851-jps","name":"JPS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T11:08:51-07:00","id":868582,"title":"","content":"I see pointing a gun to your wife as an action that could or could not trigger a breaking point and shoot to your wife as a totally different action. Both provoked by two different command: &quot;point the gun to your wife&quot; (extended action) and &quot;shoot to your wife&quot; (ISTANT ACTION). In the first case you point the gun, incur in the breaking point resolving the condition and, IN THE NEXT turn, lower your weapon. In the second case you shoot to your wife as an istant action in just one turn, incurring in the breaking point after having performed that command. The act of point the gun, in the latter case, is not an action, the action is to shoot.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 04-19-2016, 02:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T11:18:19-07:00","id":868592,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JPS</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868556#post868556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Obviously there are different interpretations here. Leetsepeak, I see your point about the fact that being put into a position where you're about to kill someone would stressful but, game wise it's only after you do it that you would have to make this breaking point roll and, imo, that's why &quot;kill your husband&quot; would be a valid command.<br /> Sure, breaking points could somewhat be subjective, somewhat. Because, by and large, we are the products of the society we live in when it comes to values and social norms. This must be adjusted to reflect specificities (I'm a soldier, I'm a gangster, I'm a homicide detective etc.) but I don't think that the adjustment should widly vary from one person to another if they both belong to the same subculture.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I agree with JPS that breaking points occur AFTER something has happened. You witness something awful, afterwords you hit the breaking point. You follow through on the command to kill your spouse, you hit the breaking point. Just considering something is not a breaking point. DOING it is the breaking point. Seeing it DONE is the breaking point.<br /> <br /> I dont think feeling horrible about something is a breaking point... Feeling horrible about the breaking point is just a result of hitting it. If that makes sense. If we get into the thinking about something and it being a crime/sin/breaking point, then we fall into the Minority Report. We get conditions like guilty or shaken as a result of the breaking point. We dont get another breaking point for feeling guilty. <br /> <br /> Agreeing that someone would have to follow through with the command &quot;Kill your husband&quot; it is within the RAW and the breaking point would occur after it was done, or you failed at it. (subdued knocked out, Hit hard enough.. because damage breaks mesmerized ) <br /> <br /> There is a lot of arguing in circles in this thread. Sorry if I am adding to it. <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T11:35:41-07:00","id":868612,"title":"","content":"Pointing a loaded firearm at another human being certainly falls under the Breaking Points of most characters. You will be arrested just for that, no need to actually fire the bullet.<br /> <br /> Also, for most people, even thinking, REALLY thinking about killing, should be a Breaking Point. Most of the time we end up flirting with the idea of killing someone and what it would be like. However, actually feeling the drive to remove someones life, specially a loved one, certainly breaks the mind, even without executing the action itself.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5446-binaly","name":"BinAly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5446_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"146"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T11:45:41-07:00","id":868622,"title":"","content":"I feel like the complaints about thoughtcrime are missing the point.<br /> <br /> If:<br /> 1.) pointing a gun at your spouse is a breaking point for you<br /> 2.) you are ordered to shoot your spouse<br /> 3.) you point a gun at your spouse<br /> <br /> Then, to borrow Vitalis's phrasing, you have DONE the thing that causes a breaking point (and have also Seen it DONE.) Right?<br /> <br /> Imagine the command was &quot;Torture your wife to death.&quot;<br /> <br /> If injuring your spouse or menacing your spouse is a breaking point - and integrity breaking points can include just harming someone or even just seeing someone be hurt - wouldn't you encounter one of those <i>well before </i>you completed the order?<br /> <br /> The alternative is a situation where ordering someone to do something <i>summarily </i>causes them to respond wildly differently than if you asked them to do it <i>stepwise</i>.<br /> <br /> Should &quot;Pick up the gun. Point it at your temple. Pull the trigger&quot; break at step two when &quot;shoot yourself in the head&quot; works fine?<br /> <br /> The fact that if someone had done this <i>willingly </i>we would by convenience collapse all of these into a single, highly-penalized breaking point seems sort of irrelevant - that's for the same reason that the game doesn't require you to roll twice when you murder someone (because you've killed someone <i>and seen </i>someone get killed, and those are both common breaking points.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i> I attack people with giant insects both </i>on <i>and </i>off <i>the court.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1653-five-eyes","name":"Five Eyes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1653_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"395"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T12:26:54-07:00","id":868664,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Five Eyes</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868622#post868622\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I feel like the complaints about thoughtcrime are missing the point.<br /> <br /> If:<br /> 1.) pointing a gun at your spouse is a breaking point for you<br /> 2.) you are ordered to shoot your spouse<br /> 3.) you point a gun at your spouse<br /> <br /> Then, to borrow Vitalis's phrasing, you have DONE the thing that causes a breaking point (and have also Seen it DONE.) Right?<br /> <br /> Imagine the command was &quot;Torture your wife to death.&quot;<br /> <br /> If injuring your spouse or menacing your spouse is a breaking point - and integrity breaking points can include just harming someone or even just seeing someone be hurt - wouldn't you encounter one of those <i>well before </i>you completed the order?<br /> <br /> The alternative is a situation where ordering someone to do something <i>summarily </i>causes them to respond wildly differently than if you asked them to do it <i>stepwise</i>.<br /> <br /> Should &quot;Pick up the gun. Point it at your temple. Pull the trigger&quot; break at step two when &quot;shoot yourself in the head&quot; works fine?<br /> <br /> The fact that if someone had done this <i>willingly </i>we would by convenience collapse all of these into a single, highly-penalized breaking point seems sort of irrelevant - that's for the same reason that the game doesn't require you to roll twice when you murder someone (because you've killed someone <i>and seen </i>someone get killed, and those are both common breaking points.)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Correct. The book states when you hit multiple ones in the same action you roll at the lowest one.<br /> <br /> While I agree with you fully. That Pointing a gun at someone might be a breaking point for HUMAN A and not B - however it would not be one for a vampire. Integrity and Humanity breaking points are hugely different.<br /> <br /> For Mesmerize I would not allow someone to give the command, &quot;Torture your wife to death&quot; because the book says 3-4 words. I would however allow &quot;torture your wife&quot; Either way, once you have started, you would hit that breaking point.<br /> <br /> <br /> Thematically I think breaking points are suppose to be major life altering events, something Important.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BinAly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868612#post868612\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pointing a loaded firearm at another human being certainly falls under the Breaking Points of most characters. You will be arrested just for that, no need to actually fire the bullet.<br /> <br /> Also, for most people, even thinking, REALLY thinking about killing, should be a Breaking Point. Most of the time we end up flirting with the idea of killing someone and what it would be like. However, actually feeling the drive to remove someones life, specially a loved one, certainly breaks the mind, even without executing the action itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>You say that most of us think about it time to time, that is true, but we dont hit breaking points for it daily. we dont run around with guilty conditions ( or the others )<br /> <br /> But when you actually get the drive and start planning it out.... the breaking point is still when it is carried out and would fall under &quot;premeditated murder&quot; I just looked over the sample breaking points for Integrity and Humanity. None of them included thoughts or feelings.. only actions or seeing the results of Actions.<br /> <br /> now if you felt the drive and picked up the phone and called a hit man.. or took out an insurance policy with the intent... that is still an action. But if you carried it out, I still would only make you roll the 1 at the lowest &quot;premeditated murder&quot; not 2. for planning and killing","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2308,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","user":"Vitalis","date":". 04-19-2016, 03:44 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T13:54:43-07:00","id":868734,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Correct. The book states when you hit multiple ones in the same action you roll at the lowest one.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think this is really for describing very specific situations wherein multiple sins are committed in the same breath. It's not the situation where:<br /> <br /> You hit a breaking point for <b>Violently Confronting Your Husband</b>. Your character has a personal breaking point against Harming Family Members. You then hit another breaking point when you two struggle and fight and the gun goes off and actually kills him. This is <b>Premeditated Murder</b>. These are two separate breaking points which are not actually occurring in the same action.<br /> <br /> <b>Compare with:</b><br /> <br /> You killed him. You noted to your Storyteller your character has a personal breaking point for <b>Killing a Family Member.</b> Your character has to roll for that. They also have to roll for <b>Premeditated Murder</b>. You take the worst penalty out of them and use that as the one to roll with. These are two different breaking points occurring in the same action, hence you roll them together. If this is still not clicking, refer to the bottom example of the supernatural creature.<br /> <br /> ****<br /> <br /> Do you see how these are two different situations?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I agree with you fully. That Pointing a gun at someone might be a breaking point for HUMAN A and not B - however it would not be one for a vampire. Integrity and Humanity breaking points are hugely different.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is exactly correct, and it's theoretically one of the problems with the Mesmerized and Dominated Conditions. Should one do it when the character checks for detachment? That seems to be the closest equivalent, but it's an ST's judgement call. It definitely gives Kindred a higher threshold for fucked up acts under Dominate than a regular joe human just subject to Mesmerism. That actually sounds fairly thematic to me, but your mileage may vary.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">For Mesmerize I would not allow someone to give the command, &quot;Torture your wife to death&quot; because the book says 3-4 words. I would however allow &quot;torture your wife&quot; Either way, once you have started, you would hit that breaking point.</div> </div> </div> </div>So here, you're halfway to understanding what I'm talking about. I've never once disagreed that Torture your wife or shoot your wife is not a valid command, but people don't actually know what the difference between a valid and an invalid command <i>are.</i><br /> <br /> As per the rules set out in Mesmerize:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vampire The Requiem Second Edition</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Once she has inflicted the Mesmerized Condition, the vampire can command the victim as an instant action. Her<br /> commands can\u2019t be longer than three or four words, and she has to be direct \u2014 her control doesn\u2019t extend to issuing vague commands. \u201cFollow me,\u201d \u201cShoot your husband,\u201d and \u201cRepeat<br /> after me\u2026\u201d are all suitable commands, while \u201cForget,\u201d \u201cSubmit,\u201d and \u201cDo my bidding\u201d have too much room for interpretation.</div> </div> </div> </div>In order for a command to be valid, it <i>only </i>has to meet these two standards<br /> <br /> 1. That it is no longer than three or four words<br /> <br /> 2. That it isn't too vague.<br /> <br /> If a command meets those two standards, it is a valid command, period.<br /> <br /> Shoot yourself <i>is </i>a valid command. So is Shoot your Husband. You can issue those commands if you succeed the check and inflict the Condition. <b>BUT </b>this is <b>ENTIRELY SEPARATE </b>from whether the command will be followed with <b>only </b>the Mesmerized Condition.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thematically I think breaking points are suppose to be major life altering events, something Important.</div> </div> </div> </div>Dude, if committing to a premeditated plan to murder your spouse is not exactly that for a normal person, I'm not sure what is.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You say that most of us think about it time to time, that is true, but we dont hit breaking points for it daily. we dont run around with guilty conditions ( or the others )</div> </div> </div> </div>But you certainly might hit that breaking point when you're standing outside the house with the car ready for her body and gun in hand. It's a dramatic mental moment of crossing the rubicon: Once you're there with your gun in hand, there's no going back to the way things were before. That's exactly the kind of thing a breaking point represents, and it is a <b>different </b>breaking point than <b>actually murdering her</b>.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But when you actually get the drive and start planning it out.... the breaking point is still when it is carried out and would fall under &quot;premeditated murder&quot; I just looked over the sample breaking points for Integrity and Humanity. None of them included thoughts or feelings.. only actions or seeing the results of Actions.<br /> <br /> now if you felt the drive and picked up the phone and called a hit man.. or took out an insurance policy with the intent... that is still an action. But if you carried it out, I still would only make you roll the 1 at the lowest &quot;premeditated murder&quot; not 2. for planning and killing</div> </div> </div> </div>Again, you're describing these things as one breaking point altogether when they are actually two different ones occuring likely in different scenes.<br /> <br /> It's the same thing as rolling for witnessing the supernatural when you see the monster crawling through the sewers, then later rolling for being attacked by the monster when, in another scene, it leaps out of the sewers and savages you.<br /> <br /> Those are not the same breaking point. They are two different ones occurring at different times.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":159,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","user":"Leetsepeak","date":". 04-19-2016, 05:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T14:19:03-07:00","id":868757,"title":"","content":"/ gives up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T15:03:36-07:00","id":868795,"title":"","content":"Mesmerize is the first dot of Dominate. It costs no Vitae to use and will more likely than not, succeed vs most Mortals. There are a wide range of benefits that can result from the issuing of simple four word commands (Let me in, give me your wallet, drop your gun, scream if people arrive). The Mesmerized condition lasts for a single scene and is resolved when one takes damage or experiences a breaking point as part of the Vampire's command. This is intentional design so that he first dot of Dominate is not all-powerful. It is intended to nudge a situation in a Vampire's favor but does not give them complete control over it. The Vampire's target will only carry out the command up to the point where it causes them to experience a breaking point.<br /> So for the example of &quot;Shoot your wife&quot;, the Vampire rolls and succeeds at using Mesmerize to inflict the condition on his target. Now let's set up the scene, the Target's wife is present and he was already holding a gun (probably to defend he and his wife). The Mesmerize condition forces him to follow the command and point the gun at his wife. At this point, the Target experiences a breaking point (Pointing an armed weapon at his wife with the intent to kill, probably a breaking point for the vast majority of people). Mesmerize resolves and he is unable to follow through with shooting his wife as he regains control and realizes what he's just done.<br /> Now let's continue the example but assume the Target also has the Dominated condition as a result of the Vampire using Iron Edict. In this example, Mesmerized resolves during the first breaking point (pointing the gun at his wife) but Dominated continues and forces the Target to experience a breaking point(Mesmerized resolves but Dominated persists in this instance). Now the Target makes a resolve + composure check resulting from experiencing a breaking point. If the Target fails this resolve+ composure check, he shoots his wife. He now experiences a final breaking point from murdering his wife and has to make an Integrity check. Otherwise, Dominated resolves and he snaps back from the Vampire's control, having nearly shot his wife.<br /> The first breaking point needs to be acknowledged because in all likelihood, this is where the command 'Shoot your wife' is the most likely to be the point where the Vampire's control over the Target ends as after this, the Target will gain a <a href=\"tel:+3\">+3</a> bonus to rolls to resist being further Mesmerized (which one would need to inflict Dominated). These rolls are important because they dictate very dramatic and scene-changing events. Breaking points in cases where the player or character has total agency over their actions should probably be combined and rolled afterward. But in this use of them, it's extremely important to the outcome of a scene to make those Breaking Point rolls.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3928,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3928-doctorpuppy","user":"doctorpuppy","date":". 04-19-2016, 06:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3928-doctorpuppy","name":"doctorpuppy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3928_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-19T19:05:19-07:00","id":868991,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868216#post868216\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Dude, you keep saying this as if I disagree. It's in the text, it's a valid use of the power. I'm not talking about whether you can command someone to do it or not, I'm only talking about the Condition <b>Mesmerized </b>and how it functions, or more specifically, how it resolves.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The only assumption the book seems to be going on is that &quot;Shoot your husband&quot; is a command that can be given in a single turn as per Mesmerize. This is true. How it interacts with <b>Mesmerized </b>and <b>Dominated </b>is the thing that bears scrutiny.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'm hoping that this time will be the time that what I'm saying gets through to you, since you keep deciding what my interpretation is rather than reading it.<br /> <br /> So let me make it clear: <b>Mesmerized is a Condition that resolves when someone encounters a breaking point. Dominated is a Condition that resolves if they succeed the Resolve + Composure roll, but does not resolve if they fail it. Ergo, the intended function is that Mesmerize, the 'low level' effect of Dominate, is the kind you use when the action is either of little consequence or is unlikely to lead to a breaking point. If you want to order somebody to kill your wife, you use Iron Edict, which modifies Mesmerize by spending 1 Vitae, because unlike Mesmerized, the Dominated Condition does not automatically resolve when they encounter the breaking point. Since the command 'shoot your husband' is under the limit described in Mesmerize, it may be applied in the 1 turn required to give a command. That is the value of placing it in the Mesmerized section, it is an example of a command that can be given by the 1 dot effect of Dominate, which does not at all engage with what happens when the Condition of Mesmerized is applied to a person due to their personal breaking points. If this difference didn't exist between the Mesmerized and Dominated Conditions, there would be no point to having both of them and describing them differently.</b><br /> <br /> So if it bears reiterating <b><i>again </i></b>hat I am saying is that when you want someone to do something that's a breaking point, you spend 1 Vitae and issue the command as per Iron Edict. That's the difference between the 1st dot and the 2nd dot. That is why they are different dots in the Discipline of Dominate.<br /> <br /> <b>Mesmerized</b> works all the time on the things its supposed to work against. Those situations where it doesn't work are usually the ones where you want to apply <b>Dominated</b>.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, we are <i>not</i> agreeing, because you seem to keep skipping over a very key portion of what I'm saying. <i>I'm</i> talking about how they wouldn't have included it as an example if it wasn't just a <i><u>'technically'</u></i> valid use of the power but an <i><u>'actual'</u></i> valid use of the power. With your interpretation, the command is only <i><u>'technically'</u></i> valid. You can give it, but in 90% of the situations in which you do it won't be followed through with to the point of relevance (aka, the point where the person shoots their husband) because it would break due to the way rules interact with your interpretations. I instead posit that what the developers actually intended was for that example to be <i><u>'actually'</u></i> valid, not just pretend to be, in which, at least in the majority of cases, the command is <i>valid to be carried through to completion</i>, sans the almost certainty that it will fail before completion due to conflicting mechanics.<br /> <br /> And this is why I think you're wrong. Because if it were not supposed to be an <i><u>'actually'</u> </i>valid use of a command the writers wouldn't have wasted the word count in the core book detailing how it was an option, or would have at the very least made <i>mention</i> of the fact that this specific example is unlikely to be of any use because by the most strict interpretation of the rules it would break the moment it was given in 90% of the situations in which it was used in because even seriously thinking about it could be a Breaking Point for 90% of the people in the world.<br /> <br /> And again, I don't believe I can (or care to try) to convince you that you're wrong, but I want it to be clear why I think you're wrong. It isn't because I don't get what you're saying, it's just that I think your interpretation goes against the intentions of the writers where it concerns the Dominate power and shouldn't be used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2309,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","user":"Ashenrogue","date":". 04-19-2016, 10:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2309-ashenrogue","name":"Ashenrogue","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1388"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T03:42:21-07:00","id":869206,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Leetsepeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868361#post868361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> normal people,</div> </div> </div> </div> Lets not go there. Normal is different for people depending on where they live, how they were raised, and a whole host of socio-biological factors. There is no such thing as &quot;normal people,&quot; and trying to set one group up as normal is prejudice by saying any of the others of millions of people are deviants. That's not a message that the Integrity system is meant to model. There are no objective breaking points for mortals because trying to establish that is bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T06:20:31-07:00","id":869265,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=868664#post868664\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You say that most of us think about it time to time, that is true, but we dont hit breaking points for it daily. we dont run around with guilty conditions ( or the others )<br /> <br /> But when you actually get the drive and start planning it out.... the breaking point is still when it is carried out and would fall under &quot;premeditated murder&quot; I just looked over the sample breaking points for Integrity and Humanity. None of them included thoughts or feelings.. only actions or seeing the results of Actions.<br /> <br /> now if you felt the drive and picked up the phone and called a hit man.. or took out an insurance policy with the intent... that is still an action. But if you carried it out, I still would only make you roll the 1 at the lowest &quot;premeditated murder&quot; not 2. for planning and killing</div> </div> </div> </div>What I do believe you are missing is that &quot;Pointing the Gun at her Head&quot; is an action. While EVERYTHING is still inside your head, you can rationalize that you would never even get close to do it. But lifting the gun and pointing it at the face of you spouse, while seeing the horror in her eyes, can easily trigger a breaking point to most people. Violence isn't limited to physical actions, you can verbaly hurt someone, a lot. Even by looking at someone in a way that hurts them, you might trigger a breaking point for you character.<br /> <br /> In fact, if we area really talking about a loved one (touchstone for a Vampire), I believe that pointing a gun (or starting any action with the possibility of Death as a result) will trigger a breaking point even before the action. Touchstones are the most important thing Vampires have connected to their Humanity. Breaking Points destroy Humanity. It makes total sense (at least in my disturbed little mind) that your Humanity will do EVERYTHING to protect the thing it values the most.<br /> <br /> This is that scene where your Sire orders you to kill your wife, and your hand stops mid-movement, trembling, while your mind struggles with the idea of really hurting her. And then he intensifies his grasp, spending Vitae for Iron Edict, and you slowly start to rise the hand, while dying inside, and finally pulls the trigger at her, and with her your own Humanity.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5446-binaly","name":"BinAly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5446_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"146"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T06:29:04-07:00","id":869269,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BinAly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869265#post869265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What I do believe you are missing is that &quot;Pointing the Gun at her Head&quot; is an action. While EVERYTHING is still inside your head, you can rationalize that you would never even get close to do it. But lifting the gun and pointing it at the face of you spouse, while seeing the horror in her eyes, can easily trigger a breaking point to most people. Violence isn't limited to physical actions, you can verbaly hurt someone, a lot. Even by looking at someone in a way that hurts them, you might trigger a breaking point for you character.<br /> <br /> In fact, if we area really talking about a loved one (touchstone for a Vampire), I believe that pointing a gun (or starting any action with the possibility of Death as a result) will trigger a breaking point even before the action. Touchstones are the most important thing Vampires have connected to their Humanity. Breaking Points destroy Humanity. It makes total sense (at least in my disturbed little mind) that your Humanity will do EVERYTHING to protect the thing it values the most.<br /> <br /> This is that scene where your Sire orders you to kill your wife, and your hand stops mid-movement, trembling, while your mind struggles with the idea of really hurting her. And then he intensifies his grasp, spending Vitae for Iron Edict, and you slowly start to rise the hand, while dying inside, and finally pulls the trigger at her, and with her your own Humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div>No, I disagre. The action is shooting. One action take one turn. If the command is to shoot, so the action is the shoot not the movement to take the gun up, starting to press the trigger and so on. You don't need even to aim. It's not the command. The command is Shoot to your fucked wife. This is the mechanic of the game. Then if you want to interpret it according to your point of view it's ok. <br /> A lot of time happens to do a stupid and impulsive action and after, thinking about it, say: &quot;shit what a stupid thing I did. I acted impulsively&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7079,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","user":"Marcus","date":". 04-20-2016, 12:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T09:15:27-07:00","id":869374,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BinAly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869265#post869265\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> What I do believe you are missing is that &quot;Pointing the Gun at her Head&quot; is an action. While EVERYTHING is still inside your head, you can rationalize that you would never even get close to do it. But lifting the gun and pointing it at the face of you spouse, while seeing the horror in her eyes, can easily trigger a breaking point to most people. Violence isn't limited to physical actions, you can verbaly hurt someone, a lot. Even by looking at someone in a way that hurts them, you might trigger a breaking point for you character.<br /> <br /> In fact, if we area really talking about a loved one (touchstone for a Vampire), I believe that pointing a gun (or starting any action with the possibility of Death as a result) will trigger a breaking point even before the action. Touchstones are the most important thing Vampires have connected to their Humanity. Breaking Points destroy Humanity. It makes total sense (at least in my disturbed little mind) that your Humanity will do EVERYTHING to protect the thing it values the most.<br /> <br /> This is that scene where your Sire orders you to kill your wife, and your hand stops mid-movement, trembling, while your mind struggles with the idea of really hurting her. And then he intensifies his grasp, spending Vitae for Iron Edict, and you slowly start to rise the hand, while dying inside, and finally pulls the trigger at her, and with her your own Humanity.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I also disagree With this.<br /> <br /> Mechanically breaking points should be after you have taken your turn, after you have done something. It is does not take your turn to point the gun. Then another turn to shoot the gun. You lift, point and shoot all in the matter of a second. It is an acceptable and VALID command that most people *should* follow through with THEN hit the breaking point stopping further commands. So I am with Marcus that the ACTION is the actual shooting. As Ashenrogue said, They would not have included this if 90% of the time the command would fail because thinking about it is a breaking point.<br /> <br /> Second Humanity is not Integrity or Morality. If we are talking about vampires considering shooting someone, then I dont think that is a breaking point. Humanity is losing yourself to the beast. Yes it is Morally wrong to point a gun at your spouse, but is it giving in to your beast? Is it losing touch with what you once were are you becoming the monster inside? If you are saying that Picking up a gun because someone could die is a humanity check.. Then a vampire just simply waking up for the day should be one too... With all the shit that could go wrong with a vampire ( Frenzy ) they are like a loaded gun walking around. Side note: No where on the list of examples does it include &quot;thinking about harming your touchstone&quot; Actually touchstones arent even on the list.<br /> <br /> I still do not think FEELINGS and THOUGHTS should be breaking points. And actions shouldn't be broken into tons of little pieces where multiple breaking points occur for the same result. ( picking up the gun 1, pointing the gun 2. Killing someone with the gun 3... it should be &quot;Killing with the gun&quot; .. that's why they have it in different places on the list. Was it premeditated or not )<br /> <br /> <br /> Adding: I think we are all forgetting something huge. When you are dominated. Your will is completely crushed. You dont put thought into what you are doing, you just do. You are a puppet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2308,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","user":"Vitalis","date":". 04-20-2016, 12:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T10:31:11-07:00","id":869415,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869269#post869269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> No, I disagre. The action is shooting. One action take one turn. If the command is to shoot, so the action is the shoot not the movement to take the gun up, starting to press the trigger and so on. You don't need even to aim. It's not the command. The command is Shoot to your fucked wife. This is the mechanic of the game. Then if you want to interpret it according to your point of view it's ok.<br /> A lot of time happens to make a stupid and impulsive action and after, thinking about it, say: &quot;shit what a stupid think I did. I acted impulsively&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>I was not talking about an Action, mechanically speaking, just about the action of lifting your hand, sorry for the confusion. Since the book doesn't adress this issue specifically, we both are talking about point of views. I believe Breaking Points can happen after ANYTHING the character does, including &quot;Pointing a Loaded Gun to Someone, with the Intention to Kill&quot;. One of my Vampire players even had something like that as a specific Breaking Point at the start of the chronicle: &quot;She had trouble understanding people that meant serious harm to others, and when she listened to her father discussing if the slaves should or should not be killed, she faced a Breaking Point&quot;.<br /> <br /> Again, I believe this is less about the specific mechanics, since the rules allow for Breaking Points anywhere you want them to be.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869269#post869269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Mechanically breaking points should be after you have taken your turn, after you have done something. It is does not take your turn to point the gun. Then another turn to shoot the gun. You lift, point and shoot all in the matter of a second. It is an acceptable and VALID command that most people *should* follow through with THEN hit the breaking point stopping further commands. So I am with Marcus that the ACTION is the actual shooting. As Ashenrogue said, They would not have included this if 90% of the time the command would fail because thinking about it is a breaking point.</div> </div> </div> </div>I may take more than one turn to aim a pistol, if you are going to discuss combat rules. I would probably go with this kind of behavior if it was an intense, &quot;Heat of the Moment&quot;, scene. However, in a calm, social scene, when we are not rolling initiative, them I would probably adopt a slower pace of action, and allow for Breaking Points to happen in between player decisions. Combat is a very messy environment that doesn't allow for &quot;overthinking&quot;, other situations might allow it for characters that have problems with the action that was forced onto them.<br /> <br /> Talking about Design Intention, I do believe they wanted to separate Dominate 1 from Dominate 2 by increasing what you can force the other person to do, but withou official word, I'm not going to push this discussion further, your point seems as valid as mine.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869269#post869269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Second Humanity is not Integrity or Morality. If we are talking about vampires considering shooting someone, then I dont think that is a breaking point. Humanity is losing yourself to the beast. Yes it is Morally wrong to point a gun at your spouse, but is it giving in to your beast? Is it losing touch with what you once were are you becoming the monster inside? If you are saying that Picking up a gun because someone could die is a humanity check.. Then a vampire just simply waking up for the day should be one too... With all the shit that could go wrong with a vampire ( Frenzy ) they are like a loaded gun walking around. Side note: No where on the list of examples does it include &quot;thinking about harming your touchstone&quot; Actually touchstones arent even on the list.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, because like I said, thinking is not acting, but literally pointing the gun, is acting. Also, the book tells the player to add his own personal Breaking Points to the list.<br /> <br /> About Humanity and Morality: No, Humanity is not Morality, but it is like Integrity, which uses the new Breaking Point system. It doesn't matter if value of your actions, just if they ressonate, or not, with the Human you once was. When the Vampire loses sense of what he was after the Embrace (His old Integrity, which now is Humanity), the Man becomes vulnerable to the Beast. If you execute actions that you would never do before being turned into a Vampire, the Beast has an easier time conquering what is left of your consciousness. If you never pointed a gun at someone when human, the beast will easily use this action to consume your soul.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869269#post869269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I still do not think FEELINGS and THOUGHTS should be breaking points. And actions shouldn't be broken into tons of little pieces where multiple breaking points occur for the same result. ( picking up the gun 1, pointing the gun 2. Killing someone with the gun 3... it should be &quot;Killing with the gun&quot; .. that's why they have it in different places on the list. Was it premeditated or not )</div> </div> </div> </div>If it works for your group, just go with it! Our experience is that FORCED feelings and thoughts are very intrusive and can easily break someones identity, which add much more Drama to the story (oh, juicy, juicy Drama). Dominate is the equivalent of rape, just psicological. Even minor forced actions, like &quot;Raise your hand&quot;, might break someone that never experience such powerlessness.<br /> <br /> About the separation of &quot;actions into tons of little pieces&quot;, it is not like that. You just roll for the worse deed in the scene. However, when things like Conditions can be resolved by testing Integrity/Humanity, it is important to understand when exactly your character breaks.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vitalis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/867157-2ed-dominate-and-breaking-point?p=869269#post869269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Adding: I think we are all forgetting something huge. When you are dominated. Your will is completely crushed. You dont put thought into what you are doing, you just do. You are a puppet.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes. Until you face a Breaking Point, which forces your mind to fight against the outside force. Maybe you win, maybe you lose and do the deed. Or maybe the Vampire uses a stronger power and forces you anyway.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5446-binaly","name":"BinAly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5446_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"146"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T15:04:33-07:00","id":869692,"title":"","content":"Humanity is not integrity at all. Now we are going to go off on another tangent. But humanity is losing touch with being human. Forgetting what it was like to have a heart beat and accepting the monster inside you. If it was entirely equatable to integrity, humans would have touchstones to remind them they are human... They are quite similar, but thematically very different... <br /> <br /> Next there is no universe where raising your hand would be a breaking point. You don't even remember what happened when you're dominated. You don't know you are helpless. You don't know you are dominated. You don't walk around feeling helpless because you don't realize anything is wrong. <br /> <br /> Dominate breaks after after you have done the breaking point.. Not before it occurs. Doing it like that takes away from RAI <br /> <br /> Again wod mechanics you lay out your intent for the turn, so no. You don't feel horrible and fight against lifting the gun. You don't wait turns to aim so you can break dominate. If the command is &quot;shoot your wife&quot;. You shoot your wife. You have reached the breaking point and are not subject to further commands, assuming you passed. If you didn't, you don't care you just shot your wife. <br /> <br /> If you start breaking down dominate commands into then it takes away from<br /> The intent of the power. <br /> <br /> I feel again, if thoughts and feelings were intended to be breaking points they would have been listed somewhere... On an example. Not a single one of them are thoughts or feelings.<br /> <br /> I am not sure about having forced feelings being a breaking point because under majesty, or even a blood bond, or false memories, you don't know that your feelings shouldn't be like that. <br /> <br /> And dominate is more like getting black out drunk where you can't quite remember what the heck you did. Unless it was really really bad ... ( breaking point. )","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2308-vitalis","name":"Vitalis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2308_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"914"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-20T15:22:37-07:00","id":869704,"title":"","content":"If I had an ST that would force breaking points for even thinking about doing something that would cause a breaking point I would find a different ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9368-princeraven","name":"PrinceRaven","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9368_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}